Subject: [#94-6-154] U.S. Tech Corps & State Schooling

Tim Starr wrote 6/25/94: <In the past, they were expected to teach reading,
writing, and arithmetic.  Today they are expected to teach social status,
dependency, and complacency.> and <Who do you think Lancaster, Pennsylvania, is
named after?>

The problem is not just state control over education through group rights
regulations, conditions tied to purse strings, and general government
incompetence in administering anything; it is also the philosophical nihilism
and cultural relativism masquerading as a concern for "equality" that reigns at
teacher's colleges throughout Western civilization.  Even private schools have
to pick from this misbegotten litter of intellectual and moral runts.
Mediocrity is the standard, because excellence is inherently anti-equality.
Truth is taught as relative, because the philosophical bent in academe is
towards meaninglessness and purposelessness.  This philosophical battle at the
teacher college level is where reform of education is most critical.  There is
purpose in life, there is meaning, excellence is the only standard.  Extropic
values are absolute not relative, they lead away from stagnant dogma and
death-worshipping nihilism.

BTW, I thought Lancaster, PA was named after the English Duke of Lancaster just
like it's sister city York, PA across the river was named after the English Duke
of York.  A tribute to the War of the Roses from colonial times.

Mary Morris wrote 6/24/94: <RJ: Get government out of education altogether.
MM: It does seem to be a logical next step. Do we need a revolution?
Or would an Ammendment be sufficient?>

Revolutions are revolting things.  "Meet the new boss... same as the old boss."

Amendments don't mean a whole lot when our Constitution no longer is supported
by the consensual purpose that was present at it's adoption.  Individual rights
specified in the Constitution have been utterly overthrown in favor of group
rights introduced by our legislators in the Congress and the Federal Judiciary.
As long as judges are legislating from the bench without accountability to the
will of the people, and regulators make law without accountability to Congress,
we will continue to live in a barbaric society.

Here in Oregon, we are trying to get around to privatizing education by reducing
taxes.  The only way to deal with oppressive government in the social welfare
state is to cut off the bureaucrat's energy flow, i.e., tax revenue.  When taxes
are cut and cut again, sooner or later the painful yelps of the teacher's and
state employee's unions grow tedious and real changes start to be proposed that
take power out of administrator's hands and give it to the consumers, the
parents.  Of course, the entropic Democrats responded by foisting Outcome-Based
Education on us statewide, where secret social value tests are given in class
that produce a visit to the parents by a social worker if the students don't
give the state-approved answers.  Parents are the only legitimate people who can
determine what intellectual content and what moral values should be taught to
their children.  This means a choice of schools, the smaller the school, the
better.  The character of the principal at each smaller school is crucial to the
success of the student's achievement (given that obstructive state and federal
regulations have been eliminated).

Here is Nietzsche's idea of what educational standards should be in place for
producing good little Ubermensch (from _Will to Power_):  "I absolutely cannot
see how one can later make up for having failed to go to a good school at the
proper time.  For this is what distinguishes the hard school as a good school
from all others:  that much is demanded; and sternly demanded; that the good,
even the exceptional, is demanded as the norm; that praise is rare, that
indulgence is nonexistent; that blame is apportioned sharply, objectively,
without regard for talent or antecedents.  What does one learn in a hard school?
Obeying and commanding."  This is the direct opposite of what our teachers are
learning to do in college these days with all their mealy-mouthed
multiculturism, self-esteem coddling, group grading and emphasis on feeling over
thinking, no great issues - keep their focus on the reeking, mud-filled valleys
not the icy, clear mountain peaks.

In ancient Greek times, the affluence and relativism of Athens was parallel to
American affluence and our McNihilism (that's purposelessness with a happy
face).  Then, as now, the skepticism derived from overemphasis on rationality
formed an educational system that "stressed knowledge more than character, and
produced masses of half-educated people who, uprooted from labor and the land,
moved about in unplaced discontent like loosened cargo in the ship of state"
(quote from Will Durant's _The Life of Greece_).  In addition, the excesses of
liberty and equality then, as now, produced teachers "training the citizens in
such fashion that they looked upon insolence as democracy, lawlessness as
liberty, impudence of speech as equality, and license to do what they pleased as
happiness" (quote from Isocrates' _Areopagiticus_).  As usual in human history,
similar conditions produce similar reactions.  The dogma and intolerance of
mediocre Political Correctness is the result of wealth and theocratic scientific
rationalism.

The solution is to starve the present degraded public educational system of
money, while building up private educational institutions from Kindergarten
through teacher's colleges that set intellectual excellence as the only
standard, and extropic purpose, meaning, and character-building as the only
viable alternative to the social values of stagnant dogma and entropic
death-worshipping.  This is a long-range project.

