From: solman@MIT.EDU
Date: Mon, 27 Jun 94 00:15:32 EDT
Subject: [#94-6-158] [#94-6-88] digital cash and free banking 

>  ) Mary Morris writes:
>  ) ....
>  ) If you really want your digicash to be anonymous, you will probably
>  ) end up paying an additional fee equal to what the institution would
>  ) get for selling that info on the open market.

This scenario assumes no competition. If there exist providers of anonymous
digital money other than your present bank, then the bank will either have
to offer anonymous banking, pay you substantial compensation (in the form
of higher interest rates?) for using the information about you, or lose
your business.

Selling your privacy can be an enourmously profitable activity. It is
difficult to believe that after the widespread introduction of digital
cash into our relatively free economy that banks will not be forced to pay
a premium for your privacy.

I would suggest that as we recieve more and more of our information
via electronic means, and as the agent based filtering of that
information becomes more common, banking will become entirely
anonymous and people will sell their privacy on personalized
electronic agent based markets in which advertisers (or the electronic
representatives thereof) bid for your attention.

Allowing banks to purchase your privacy is inefficient.

Cheers,

Jason W. Solinsky

