> For starters, in all U. S. jurisdictions, you must get official
> permission from the state to open a new bank. Such permission is very
> difficult to get in most states. No such permission is required (in
> most states) to open a new barter exchange or commodity depository.
> So backing digital cash with anything of value other than fiat
> currency would expose one to far less bureaucratic oversight.

How does the US decide whether or not a transaction is occuring within
its jurisdiction? If you take the obvious step of seting up a distributed
electronic bank (important anyway since nobody wants to be denied access
to all of their money just because a few network connections are down),
you can set things up so that the transaction can be processed in any of
a dozen. I can think of several electronic protocols by which you can 
simultaneously process the transaction in all of the banks, but only do
the actual closing of the transaction in one location. You can delete
the identity of the location at which the transaction was actually
performed or store it in a database in the Caymans. So where did the
transaction take place? Cyberspace. Which district court has authority
there?

