From: sullivan@blaze.cs.jhu.edu (Gregory Sullivan)
Date: Mon, 27 Jun 94 03:28:16 EDT
Subject: [#94-6-160] Scenarios: What happens when nanotechnology appears? 

Mike Price suggests adding a scenario:

>8) All life will be annihilated.  Life will have to re-evolve.

>I mean, it must be a possibility, no?

I believe it certainly is a possibility - unfortunately.  (For the purposes
of this discussion I am ignoring non-Earth life - if it exists.)  I do not
wish to depress people.  The possibility of the annihilation of human life or
all life is certainly grim. It may seem an impossibility to some and I
certainly hope it does not occur.  The Extropian viewpoint as described by
Max More is optimistic and I share that viewpoint but I do not think that
that predisposition implies a blinkered viewpoint.  If one wishes to avert a
terrible event it makes sense to talk about it in my opinion.

Drexler's comments on the classic gray goo scenario are relevant.

Excerpt from Engines of Creation pg 172-173

  ``Plants'' with ``leaves'' no more efficient than today's solar cells could
out compete real-plants, crowding the biosphere with inedible foliage.
Tough, omnivorous ``bacteria'' could out-compete real bacteria:  they could
spread like blowing pollen, replicate swiftly, and reduce the biosphere to
dust in a matter of days.  Dangerous replicators could easily be to tough,
small, and rapidly spreading to stop - at least if we made no preparation.
We have trouble enough controlling viruses and fruit flies.
  Among the cognescenti of nanotechnology, this threat has become known as
the ``gray goo problem''. Though masses of uncontrolled replicators need not
be gray or gooey, the term ``gray goo'' emphasizes that replicators able to
obliterate life might be less inspiring than a single species of crabgrass.

End of excerpt

In ``Unbounding the Future'' Drexler et al rename the scenario above to be
the Star Trek Scenario.  He argues convincingly that it is unlikely to occur
due to an accident. He uses the following humorous analogy ``.. the idea of a
replicator `escaping' and replicating in the wild will be as absurd as the
notion of an automobile going feral and refueling itself from tree sap.''

If life is destroyed I do not think it will happen because of an accident in
a peace-time situation.  It will probably happen because of deliberate acts
with unintended consequences in a war-time situation.

The gray goo described above works primarily by displacing food supplies of
living creatures and by out-competing natural creatures for environmental
niches.  A more aggressive gray goo might supplement this activity by
attempting to actively disassemble other entities - molecule by molecule or
by using explosions, heat, projectiles etc. Of course, the basic functional
units of the gray goo can vary in size and can certainly be larger than
bacteria or plants.

Consider the following simplified situation. Life has not yet moved off of
the planet and the world population has aligned itself into multiple warring
factions. Two of the factions each release gray-goo-like weapons
specifically targeted at all the opposition parties.  Each of the two
parties can effectively defend itself against its own carefully designed
gray goo; however, each of the two parties is unable to defend itself
against the gray goo of its adversary. All the other parties succumb to one
or both of the gray-goo-like weapons. More realistically, perhaps, a
sequence of weapons of varying capabilities will be designed and released by
many parties. It is possible that all parties - even non-participants - will
fall prey to one or more of the weapons.

I suspect the weapons will probably be designed to knock-out human-level
cognitive entities.  The desire to knock-out a supposed ``enemy'' is a sad
but very believable human motivation.  I can not see much purpose to
building a weapon which knocks-out your ``enemy'' and gratuitously destroys
the biosphere.  Why bother to out-compete and/or disassemble every last
plant or bacterium?  Should the weapon travel down to the deep sea vents to
destroy the life-forms there?  On the other hand, the weapons may have to
work so thoroughly that the destruction of the biosphere is a grotesque form
of unintended collateral damage.

Finally, the goo(s) themselves might be considered alternative life-forms.
To fit the scenario in which all life is annihilated the goo(s) would have to
eventually self-destruct or ``die out''. If a goo is a weapon it might
have a built in self-destruct mode after it recognizes that it has completed
its task.

Why didn't I include the total destruction of life scenario?  Mainly,
because it was too similar to scenario one and because of the ``deep sea vent
bacteria argument''.  There are myriad scenarios. Pick your favorite.

What can we do?  Drexler suggests international cooperation and tight
multi-lateral international governmental controls on the development of
nanotechnology.  He favors open civilian development of nanotech. Despite
the overall upbeat tone of Unbounding the Future it is clear that Drexler
fears ``a sickening slide into a new arms race.'' 

Governmental controls appear to be incompatible with the main sentiments
expressed on the Extropian list over the last ten months I have been
perusing it.  Some Extropians are very ``anti-state'' and would like to see
the dissolution of the ``state'' others want a weak state and maximal
laissez-faire.  (See Friedman Machinery of Freedom). 

Would someone like to offer an alternative vision to that offered by Drexler
which avoids the dangers of nanotech (e.g. the destruction of life) but does
not rely on state power?

  Gregory Sullivan

